Z||000003||theogre||05-21-2000||02:54 PM||||If you compair front engine cars to fiero you'll see why many of them need bracing. I can't see any Fiero needing it for any kind of normal use or even racing in most cases.<P>In most front engine cars the strut/shock towers are a foot or more from both the firewall and the radiator frame. The large expances of relativly flat sheet metal flex considerably in a hard turn which let the top of the tower fall inward changing steering geometry.<P>Fiero's towers are about a foot from the firewall but are tied to the trunk wall. The trunk wall is not just a flat pannel. If you look behind the trunk carpet you'll see it does have rinforcement enough to significantly stiffen the wall. This makes it very unlikely the towers can move any amount as to cause a handling issue. Mayby the increased mass of a V8 could use the extra brace but that would be it. I don't think even a beafed up V6 should need it.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Pretender||05-22-2000||03:09 AM||||Then why do they make them for mr2's and nsx's? ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Phil||05-22-2000||06:46 AM||||The after market bolt on (especially the rice stuff) equipment business is a multi BILLION dollar market. They will make anything that they think can be sold.||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||05-22-2000||09:36 AM||||What phil said. Ricers will buy almost anything some other ricer tells them they need. I've seen all sorts of crap bolted to them.<P>Not to many things funnier, or more annoying, than watching some rice burning junk heap pulling into the middle of a hundred spaces full of detroit iron on cruise night. The 1/2 way smart ones don't stay too long. They can't take the fart can comments for very long.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Pretender||05-23-2000||12:56 AM||||So your saying that the strut tower braces don't help at all even on the imports? They have been tested and proven to reduce body roll and provide better cornering/handeling.<P>On another note: You guys poke and make fun of the ricers way too much, Why do you think the factory mr2's can out handle our fieros? because of simple little things like these. I'm getting half sick of you guys bashing the imports when i know and you all know there just about all faster then our cars and with a few simple suspension upgrades they can out handle us too. Ogre, When's the last time you took your 4 banger fiero to an import cruise and tried to race a crx or integra? probably never because you couldn't handle the fart can comments for very long either....||||reg||11
Z||000008||theogre||05-23-2000||04:10 PM||||The question becomes who did the testing? the part makers? one of those so called indepenant labs no-one ever heard of? It just might stiffen the body but then the bodys of most pacific rim imports are so thin that anything is an improvment. The number of truly good pacrim cars is very small. (This includes allot of imports by GM Ford and Chrysler. Ever seen a ford Festiva or Geo Metro after a wreck? I have. even a tiny wreck totals these cars and results in major injuries.)<P>lets get this straigh right here. we're picking on a class of juvinile owners of baddly altered import cars. Not all import cars or even import car parts. (Hell my 86 dodge van has a very nice mitsubishi motor in it with around 150,000 miles on it.)<P>most ricers beg to be picked on. noisy  exhausts with noisy distorted sterios that make all the body pannels buzz. cars with the suspensions chopped out or clamped down to the point they split ball joints in the middle of the road with no warning. So low that when they aren't broken they are tearing the ****  out of the roads my taxes pay for. Do they go faster than my 87SC? yes. so what. most of them will also fry the motor in rather short order.<P>I don't have to worry about fart can comments. I don't have one of those noise making pieces of trash on my car and no-one that lives in this house ever will. Fact is those devices are illegal in most states but states have to decide if they are going to chase noisy cars or fight real crimes. Most people would rather the drunk drivers and drugs are chased.<P>Race? why? so I can get hit by some incompetent ass or watch them hit some inocent bystander? I've had more than my fill of cleaning up the messes after some pair of nitwits went flying down a road and killed themselves and others. And no, this remark isn't directed just at the ricers. It's directed at ALL street racers. This is why you'll never see me posting in street race threads in the general forum. I rarely even open those threads. I just love having my insurace rates climb every year becuase of people who shouldn't even be allowed on the road. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Jay||05-23-2000||04:58 PM|||| I agree with theogre.  Racing should be done at a track not on the street!  However, I have a Northstar in my Fiero and I'm almost certain that a strut tower bar will help me out.  Even if it doesn't, I think it looks cool, especially if it is polished up nice.  For stock 4 or 6 cyl cars, you don't need it, but if yu like it, buy it.  Hell, half the stuff we put on cars are for looks anyways!!||||reg||1
Z||000010||theogre||05-23-2000||06:04 PM||||Jay, With a big 8 it may help. The 8 is both heavier as well as producing far more torque. Between the two a v8 could flex the body more than normal. The biggest thing to do with an 8 is put at least polly cradle bushings in. The OE cradle bushings just can't take an 8, especially if they are old. If you are having rear end wander I'd do some testing of the cradle and engine mount setup before adding anything else.<P>what you do to check cradle shift is get some clean flat sheetmetal, anything handy, and coat it with layout blue. Then you attach them secury to the cradle. You want to make a plate for each side an the middle of the craddle.<P>The next part is to make some scribes. heavy coat hanger wire works good. (Heavy ones not those cheap dry cleaner ones) attach the wire to the body and bend it so there is a tip touching the blue dyed plate you just put on the cradle. keep the wires as short as you can to prevent them bending.<P>If the cradle is moving, especially if it's moving one end more than the other you'll get all sorts of nasty handling problems. If the cradle is moving unevenly you have to look at both the bushings and the engine mounting system.<P>The ones on the sides will tell you if the craddle is moving unevenly front to back. The one in the middle you are watching mostly for sideways motion.<P>This isn't an item I'd do for looks only. often get's in the way really fast when you work on the engine. You want to make sure you get a well thought out one or about the third time you have to remove it to work you're likely to leave it out.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 05-23-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||Sillhouette.com||05-23-2000||06:43 PM||||Have you seen an MR2 lately. The reason that fieros don't need strut tower braces is because they are already there in the form of a trunk brace. (someone already stated this) The MR2 does not have a trunk in the back so close to the strut like the fiero. That is why a strut tower brace maybe helps on those cars.  <P>As far as handeling..... RSM rebuilt an MR2 with the turbocharged engine in it(new body style eibach lowering springs Koni adjustable struts and a world of greedy turbo stuff) and when we tested it I drove shot gun in a lowered Fiero with KYB and front and rear sway bars.  We got a little frisky on the track with the cars and guess what the MR2 could not even touch the handling abilities of the FIERO maybe a stock fiero but a lowered one no way.   <P>Later||||reg||1
